Discourse:
"A ruler ought to guard against desires before they sprout."

Edict, Mandate, and Memorial [Candidates are tested on one of these]:
Imitation of a Han edict reiterating the regulations on carriages and dress [13th year of the Yongping era (70 CE)];
Imitation of a Tang mandate appointing Zhang Yue 張說 concurrently as Academician of the Academy of Gathered Worthies (Jixian yuan xueshi) [16th year of the Kaiyuan era (728 CE)];
Imitation of a Song memorial of gratitude from the grand councilors upon being shown the Imperially Composed Discourse on "To Be a Ruler Is Difficult, to Be a Minister Is Not Easy" [5th year of the Xiangfu era (1012 CE)].

Legal Judgments [Five items]:
Auditing official documents and files;
Fraudulently concealing taxable land and grain;
Violating regulations regarding dress and dwellings;
Postal couriers delaying beyond their scheduled time;
Privately casting copper coins.

Third Session

Five Policy Questions

Question: From the eras of the Yellow Emperor, Yu [Shun], the Ji [Zhou], and the Liu [Han] onwards, worthy and sage rulers have arisen in succession; there were none who did not reverently offer sacrifices to vast Heaven and exalt the Uncrowned King [Confucius]. Regarding the canons of the lèi sacrifice to Heaven, the burnt-offering (chái), and the sacrifice from afar to mountains and rivers (wàng), …
